=head1 NAME

dircmp.pl  -  directory comparison

=head1 SYNOPSIS

dircmp  d1  d2

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Will compare all items in subtrees d1 and d2, and will identify
what items are the same, different or unique.  The output
resembles:

  common     dir   dir_present_in_both
  same       file  file_same_in_both
  unique     dir   d1/dir_present_in_d1_only
  unique     file  file_present_in_d1_only
  different  file  file_different



=head2 Limitations:

 1. There is no cleanup made for user in paths such as:
      .///./dir_1///.///.

=head2 Differences with Solaris "dircmp" (/usr/bin/dircmp):

 1. If the two subtrees contain links to an absent file (eg. a broken link
    that points to nothing), they will appear nowhere in the dircmp output.
    Because this was felt to be misleading, in the isdc_dircmp_dircmp.pl
    they appear as "same" files.
 2. A common subdirectory is identified as "same" by dircmp, but "common" by 
    isdc_dircmp_dircmp.pl.  This was decided because it appears misleading to
    identify as "same" when the contents may be different.


   
=head1 OPTIONS

--help  Print this info

--h

-h

=head1 RETURN VALUE

0 - no different nor unique files found (or successful completion of -h)

1 - different or unique files found

2 - user error (eg. dir does not exist)

3 - coding anomaly detected (please notify the author)
    - unexpected "unix cmp" error
    - encountered file not of type "file" or "dir"
    - encountered file present more than 1 time in each dir


=head1 EXAMPLES

1. To get help:
dircmp.pl --help

2. To compare two directories just below the current one:
dircmp.pl  d1 d2

=head1 ENVIRONMENT

This tool assumes installation in the directory pointed to by environment variable $DIRCMP_INSTALL_DIR.

